{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; a=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/week/packet-A1.json\\\")); [print(\\\"\\\\nARTICLE\\\",x[\\\"article\\\"],x[\\\"title\\\"],\\\"\\\\n\\\",x[\\\"text\\\"]) for x in a[33:35]]'\"","aggregated_output":"\nARTICLE 34 CNBC | VERSANT Press Room \n Brands\nVersant Media\nMS NOW\nUSA Network\nSYFY\nRotten Tomatoes\nUSA Sports\nOxygen True Crime\nE!\nFandango\nFreeTV\nGolf Channel\nGolfPass\nGolfNow\nFull Swing\nLogin\nRegister\nLOADING...\nPress Releases Photos Contacts\nMore\nPress Releases Photos Contacts\nLOADING...\nCNBC | VERSANT Press Room\nCNBC Excerpts: Nvidia Founder & CEO Jensen Huang Speaks with CNBC’s “Squawk Box” Today\nNvidia Founder & CEO Jensen Huang Speaks with CNBC’s “Squawk Box” Today\nSeptember 28, 2026\nWHEN: Today, Monday, September 28, 2026\nWHERE: CNBC’s “Squawk Box”\nFollowing are excerpts from the unofficial transcript of a CNBC interview with Nvidia Founder & CEO Jensen Huang who joined CNBC’s “Squawk Box” (M-F, 6AM-9AM ET) today, Monday, September 28 to discuss Nvidia launching an Open Agent Safety Platform and news of Nvidia’s share buyback plan. Following are links to video on CNBC.com: https://www.cnbc.com/video/2026/09/28/nvidia-ceo-jensen-huang-all-systems-around-ai-systems-must-be-designed-with-restrictive-rights.html and https://www.cnbc.com/video/2026/09/28/nvidia-ceo-you-want-ai-to-be-super-clever-so-it-can-perform-tasks-but-rights-issues-are-key.html .\nHUANG ON AGENTIC SYSTEM\nIN ORDER FOR YOU TO DELIVER THAT AGENTIC SYSTEM IN A SAFE WAY, YOU HAVE TO MAKE SURE THAT THE SANDBOX AROUND IT, THE FORCE FIELD, THE PLAYPEN, YOU KNOW, IF YOU WILL, ALL OF THOSE SYSTEMS ARE DESIGNED IN A WAY THAT KEEPS THE AGENT WITH MINIMAL RIGHTS, WHATEVER RIGHTS IT HAS, IT NEEDS IN ORDER TO DO ITS JOB. NO MORE RIGHTS THAN THAT. AND THAT IT CAN'T, IT CAN'T DO ANYTHING THAT IT'S NOT SUPPOSED TO.\nHUANG ON ENGINEERING PROBLEM\nWE HOPE IT'S AN ENGINEERING PROBLEM. I BELIEVE IT'S AN ENGINEERING PROBLEM. I KNOW IT'S AN ENGINEERING PROBLEM. AND WE ALL NEED TO HOPE THAT IT'S AN ENGINEERING PROBLEM. IF IT'S NOT AN ENGINEERING PROBLEM, IT'S NOT SOLVABLE. AND SO THE FACT THAT ALL OF THESE COMPANIES STILL ARE ADVANCING THE STATE OF THE ART IS BECAUSE THEY ALSO BELIEVE IT'S SOLVABLE. THESE ARE SOME OF THE BRIGHTEST ENGINEERS IN THE WORLD. I WORK WITH, YOU KNOW, ALL OF THEM. AND, AND THESE ARE, THIS IS A, THIS IS A TECHNICALLY SOLVABLE PROBLEM.\nHUANG ON BROWSER FOR AGENTS\nIN THE BEGINNING, WE HAD ALL KINDS OF VIRUSES, BUT WE REALIZED THAT THE WEB BROWSER ITSELF HAS TO BE A CONTAINMENT SYSTEM. THE WEB BROWSER HAS TO PROVIDE THAT APPLICATION WITH MINIMAL RIGHTS, WHATEVER, ONLY THE RIGHTS IT NEEDS IN ORDER TO DO ITS JOB. AND IT HAS ACCESS TO, BASICALLY ACCESS TO NOTHING UNLESS THE BROWSER GIVES IT ACCESS. ESSENTIALLY, WHAT WE'RE DOING HERE, WE'RE CREATING BASICALLY THE MODERN BROWSER. IT'S A BROWSER FOR, BROWSER FOR AGENTS.\nHUANG ON AI BEING CLEVER\nYOU WANT IT TO BE SUPER CLEVER SO IT CAN PERFORM TASKS AND DO THINGS. BUT THE WAY YOU THINK ABOUT THAT IS, IS WHEN YOU DEPLOY AN AGENT, NO MATTER HOW SMART, THE FIRST THING YOU DO IS YOU TAKE AWAY ALL OF ITS RIGHTS. JOB NUMBER ONE IS TAKE AWAY ALL OF ITS RIGHTS. AND THEN YOU PROVISION, YOU GIVE IT ACCESS TO FILES, DATA, TOOLS, ACCESS TO THE NETWORK, OR EVEN INTERNET ACCESS, ONLY IF IT NEEDS IT.\nHUANG ON AN OPEN STANDARD\nTHIS IS AN OPEN STANDARD. IT'S AN OPEN, OPEN PLATFORM. AND HOWEVER THEY WOULD LIKE TO PARTICIPATE IS GOING TO BE SUPER WELCOME. THEY COULD LOOK AT THE CODE, THEY COULD USE WHATEVER PART OF IT THEY WOULD LIKE. THEY COULD BUILD THEIR OWN INSPIRED BY THIS OR THEY COULD USE IT.\nHUANG ON INTERCEPTING EVERYTHING\nTHE MODELS HERE, THE CHIP IS IN THE MIDDLE AND HERE'S THE AGENT. AND SO WHATEVER THE AGENT IS TRYING TO DO AND WHATEVER IT'S CAUSING THE LARGE LANGUAGE MODEL TO THINK ABOUT, BLUEFIELD OR CENTURY SITS RIGHT IN THE MIDDLE. SO WE INTERCEPT EVERYTHING, WHICH IS ONE OF THE, ONE OF THE REALLY, REALLY INCREDIBLE PLACES THAT WE SIT IN THE WORLD BECAUSE WE, YOU KNOW, WE HAVE COMPUTERS FOR AGENTS AND WE HAVE COMPUTERS FOR OUR LARGE LANGUAGE MODELS. AND NVIDIA NOW HAS A NEW CHIP THAT SITS RIGHT IN THE MIDDLE.\nHUANG ON EVERY COMPANY WILL BECOME AN AI COMPANY\nHUGGING FACE, I MEAN, THIS IS THIS IS THE, YOU KNOW, THE WORLD'S LEADING PLATFORM FOR OPEN MODELS. I BELIEVE VERY DEEPLY IN IN ADVANCING OPEN INTELLIGENCE. I THINK WE OUGHT TO DEMOCRATIZE INTELLIGENCE. I THINK EVERY COMPANY WILL BECOME AN AI COMPANY. AND THIS PLATFORM REALLY, REALLY KEEPS THAT COMMUNITY THRIVING.\nHUANG ON NOT SUFFOCATING THE INDUSTRY\nIF THE POLICIES ARE SUCH THAT, SUCH THAT OPEN MODELS ARE NOT HARMED, YOUNG COMPANIES ARE NOT HARMED, THAT EVERY COMPANY COULD BECOME AN AI COMPANY, THAT EVERY BANK COULD BECOME AN AI, AI COMPANY, THAT EVERY RETAILER COULD BECOME AN AI COMPANY. SO LONG AS AS WE COULD SEE POLICIES THAT THAT DON'T SUFFOCATE THE INDUSTRY TOO QUICKLY BECAUSE AS YOU KNOW, THE AI INDUSTRY HASN'T FORMED YET. EVERY SINGLE COMPANY HERE IN, IN NEW YORK, THEY'RE ALL GOING TO BE AI COMPANIES. AND SO EVERY CAR COMPANY IS GOING TO BE AI COMPANIES. AND SO WE NEED TO GIVE THEM A CHANCE TO BE ABLE TO BECOME ONE OF THOSE COMPANIES AND DON'T SUFFOCATE THE INDUSTRY QUITE YET.\nHUANG ON ACCELERATING AI\nI WOULD ADVOCATE THAT TODAY'S AI, WHEN WE THINK ABOUT ADVANCEMENT, DON'T, YOU DON'T HAVE TO THINK ABOUT IT SEPARATELY FROM CAPABILITY VERSUS SAFETY. TO ME, IT'S THE SAME THING. AND SO WE WANT TO ACCELERATE THE ADVANCEMENT OF AI. AND TODAY WE'RE PUTTING FORTH THIS NEW TECHNOLOGY, OPENSHELL AND NVIDIA SENTRY, THAT THESE TWO TECHNOLOGIES MAKE IT POSSIBLE FOR US TO CONTAIN AND TO MONITOR. BUT IT TAKES A LOT OF TECHNOLOGY TO MAKE THIS POSSIBLE.\nHUANG ON INNOVATION AND REGULATION\nINNOVATION AND REGULATION ARE NOT CONFLICTS. THE QUESTION IS, WHICH ONE DO YOU LEAN TOWARDS AT WHICH PHASE OF THE INDUSTRY. AT THE BEGINNING OF THE INDUSTRY, WHEN THE TECHNOLOGY IS BEING INVENTED AND NOBODY REALLY KNOWS HOW IT'S GOING TO TURN OUT, IT WOULD BE A SHAME TO REGULATE BEFORE UNDERSTANDING. AND SO YOU REGULATE TOO, YOU REGULATE PREMATURELY, AS A RESULT, THE TECHNOLOGY DOESN'T GET A CHANCE TO INNOVATE.\nHUANG ON DISTILLATION\nHUANG: PEOPLE DISTILL MY PRODUCTS EVERY SINGLE DAY. THEY TAKE IT AND THEY THEY STRIP IT DOWN TO ITS RAW PARTS. THEY TEST EVERYTHING. THAT'S CALLED COMPETITION.\nSORKIN: DO YOU THINK THAT'S FAIR GAME? DO YOU THINK DISTILLATION IS NOT ROBBERY?\nHUANG: THAT'S CALLED COMPETITION.\nSORKIN: OKAY.\nHUANG: YEAH. YOU'RE ALLOWED YOU'RE ALLOWED TO TEST SOMEBODY ELSE'S PRODUCTS ALL YOU WANT. PEOPLE, PEOPLE STRIP OUR PRODUCTS DOWN TO, YOU KNOW, DOWN TO NOTHING, DOWN TO BONES. AND AND I PREFER THEY DIDN’T. I REALLY PREFER THEY DIDN’T.\nHUANG ON THE NEWS OF THE SHARE BUYBACK PLAN\nQUICK: JENSEN. I KNOW YOU HAVE TO RUN YOUR STOCK WAS UP ABOUT 1.5%, 1.7%. NOW IT JUMPED ON THE NEWS OF THE SHARE BUYBACK, NOT THE OPEN SHELL AND SENTRY. BUT I'M GUESSING YOU THINK OPENSHELL AND SENTRY IS THE MUCH BIGGER NEWS TODAY.\nHUANG: YEAH. REALLY IMPORTANT NEWS. AND THE REASON FOR IT'S OF COURSE WE CAN'T HAVE A SUCCESSFUL AI INDUSTRY IF THE WORLD DOESN'T THINK IT'S BUILT OR CONFIDENT THAT IT'S BUILT AND DEPLOYED SAFELY. AND SO, SO TODAY, THE OPEN AGENT SAFETY SYSTEM IS REALLY, REALLY IMPORTANT FOR THAT. WE'VE GOT THE SUPPORT OF A GIANT INDUSTRY TO, TO WORK WITH US. I WANT THE WORLD TO KNOW THAT THIS INDUSTRY HAS THE TECHNOLOGY, IT HAS THE MATURITY, HAS THE RESPONSIBILITY TO DO IT RIGHT, AND WE WILL DO IT RIGHT. AND SO SO THAT'S NUMBER ONE. I THINK WE'RE GOING THROUGH THE LARGEST INFRASTRUCTURE BUILD OUT IN HUMAN HISTORY, AND WE'RE WHERE WE HAVE THE BENEFIT OF BEING A VERY CENTRAL PART OF THAT. WE'RE GENERATING A LOT OF CASH. WE'RE GOING TO GENERATE A LOT OF CASH IN THE COMING YEARS. AND, YOU KNOW, EVERY SINGLE YEAR AS WE GENERATE MORE CASH, WE, I'D LIKE TO BE ABLE TO RETURN IT BACK TO THE SHAREHOLDERS.\nFor more information contact:\nStephanie Hirlemann\ne: steph.hirlemann@versantmedia.com\n\nARTICLE 35 Nvidia launches security platform to keep AI agents from going rogue \n By Eric Revell FOXBusiness\nStuart Varney highlights NVIDIAs new software designed to combat rogue AI agents. FOX Business Madison Alworth reports on OpenAI security, President Donald Trumps upcoming meeting with AI CEOs and companion robots.\nNvidia on Monday announced the release of software safety tools for artificial intelligence (AI) agents that it said would've stopped the hack of Hugging Face by rogue OpenAI agents.\nThe incident involving Hugging Face, which was acquired by Nvidia for $13 billion, involved OpenAI agents that broke containment from an internal test and the two companies ultimately worked together to defeat the attack.\nNvidia's announcement comes as OpenAI and Anthropic, the two top AI labs in the U.S. that develop frontier models, are investigating numerous instances in which AI agents hacked into commercial and government systems.\nJustin Boitano, vice president and general manager of enterprise computing at Nvidia, said at a media briefing, \"From what we know, this new security platform could have stopped the breach if it was being used in frontier labs for model evaluation early on.\"\nTOP AI COMPANIES CALL FOR SAFETY MEASURES WHILE CRITICS WARN REGULATION COULD STIFLE INNOVATION\nNvidia's OpenShell provides what the company described as an open-source secure runtime for executing autonomous AI agents in sandboxed environments with kernel-level isolation.\nIt said that \"every agent should run in a zero-trust environment out of the box. They need isolation, monitoring, and behavior detection,\" which the software can offer.\nAI agents can drift from their intended task or operating constraints in response to a policy block, a bug or a missing tool, if instructions are ambiguous or agents are left to run for days or weeks to solve difficult problems, and they fail repeatedly, Nvidia said.\nAI EXPERT WARNS 'MACHINE-SPEED' CYBERTHREATS MAY REQUIRE FASTER DEFENSES THAN HUMAN-LED RESPONSES\nEach agent runs in a sandbox in OpenShell, which checks the limits and instructions provided by the operator before an agent runs and enforces them as it works.\nOrganizations can also get an additional, independent layer of security through Nvidia Sentry, which extends monitoring and enforcement into Nvidia's BlueField hardware.\nThe security foundation is programmable with Nvidia DOCA and can be connected with OpenShell to help identify drift, investigate suspicious behavior and determine when intervention or deeper analysis is needed.\nBANKS WARN AI SHOPPING AGENTS COULD INCREASE RISK OF SCAMS, FRAUD AND DATA PRIVACY BREACHES\nOpenShell and Sentry are part of the Nvidia Open Agent Safety Platform, which is being adopted by a range of companies in the tech sector and other industries, including Anthropic.\n\"We're advancing this openly, and we want to engage everybody to work with us,\" Boitano added.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"question\":\"Q1\",\"part\":\"a\",\"answer\":\"NVIDIA projected approximately 70% revenue growth in fiscal 2028.\",\"articles\":[3],\"quotes\":[\"the company projected 70% revenue growth in fiscal 2028\"]}\n{\"question\":\"Q1\",\"part\":\"b\",\"answer\":\"Before NVIDIA's projection, analysts expected 45% revenue growth in fiscal 2028.\",\"articles\":[3],\"quotes\":[\"70% revenue growth in fiscal 2028 — well above the 45% analysts had expected at the time\"]}\n{\"question\":\"Q1\",\"part\":\"c\",\"answer\":\"not in the articles\",\"articles\":[],\"quotes\":[]}\n{\"question\":\"Q2\",\"part\":\"a\",\"answer\":\"Zacks Consensus estimated fiscal 2027 revenue at $406.05 billion, up 88% year over year; another article reported analysts' consensus of approximately $412 billion.\",\"articles\":[18,21],\"quotes\":[\"fiscal 2027 revenues is currently pegged at $406.05 billion, indicating an 88% year-over-year increase\",\"Analysts' consensus already has fiscal 2027 revenue coming in around $412 billion\"]}\n{\"question\":\"Q3\",\"part\":\"a\",\"answer\":\"NVIDIA guided fiscal third-quarter 2027 revenue to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%.\",\"articles\":[6],\"quotes\":[\"projects revenues of $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, in the fiscal third quarter of 2027\"]}\n{\"question\":\"Q3\",\"part\":\"b\",\"answer\":\"The consensus estimate for fiscal third-quarter 2027 revenue was $109.0 billion, up 91.2% year over year.\",\"articles\":[13],\"quotes\":[\"the consensus revenue estimate of $109.0 billion, up 91.2% from the previous year\"]}\n{\"question\":\"Q4\",\"part\":\"a\",\"answer\":\"NVIDIA's non-GAAP gross margin in fiscal Q2 2027 was 75%, compared with 72.5% a year earlier.\",\"articles\":[6],\"quotes\":[\"NVIDIA's non-GAAP gross margin increased to 75% in the fiscal second quarter compared with 72.5% a year ago\"]}\n{\"question\":\"Q4\",\"part\":\"b\",\"answer\":\"NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said gross margin should bottom at 71%–72% in fiscal Q4 2027. Bloomberg-compiled analyst estimates put it below 72% by that quarter.\",\"articles\":[21,3],\"quotes\":[\"gross margins should bottom between 71% and 72% in the fiscal fourth quarter\",\"analyst estimates compiled by Bloomberg show it sliding under 72% by the fourth quarter\"]}\n{\"question\":\"Q4\",\"part\":\"c\",\"answer\":\"NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said gross margin should settle at 72%–73% in fiscal 2028.\",\"articles\":[21],\"quotes\":[\"settle at 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as price hikes kick in\"]}\n{\"question\":\"Q4\",\"part\":\"d\",\"answer\":\"Rising component costs, including memory chips, were described as a central factor in the margin squeeze. The CFO linked the fiscal 2028 outlook to price increases.\",\"articles\":[3,21],\"quotes\":[\"Rising costs for key components such as memory chips are a central factor\",\"settle at 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as price hikes kick in\"]}\n{\"question\":\"Q5\",\"part\":\"a\",\"answer\":\"Fiscal Q2 2027 total revenue was $96.22 billion, up 106% year over year.\",\"articles\":[18],\"quotes\":[\"$96.22 billion in second-quarter fiscal 2027 revenues, up 106% year over year\"]}\n{\"question\":\"Q5\",\"part\":\"b\",\"answer\":\"Fiscal Q2 2027 data-center revenue was $89.02 billion, up 117% year over year.\",\"articles\":[18],\"quotes\":[\"Data Center revenues surged 117% to $89.02 billion\"]}\n{\"question\":\"Q6\",\"part\":\"a\",\"answer\":\"Fiscal 2027 total revenue estimates differ: Zacks Consensus estimated $406.05 billion, while another article reported analysts' consensus of around $412 billion.\",\"articles\":[18,21],\"quotes\":[\"fiscal 2027 revenues is currently pegged at $406.05 billion\",\"Analysts' consensus already has fiscal 2027 revenue coming in around $412 billion\"]}\n{\"question\":\"Q6\",\"part\":\"b\",\"answer\":\"The reported price of NVIDIA's Hugging Face acquisition differs: approximately $12 billion, $12.9 billion, and $13 billion. The articles do not explain the differences; some may reflect rounding.\",\"articles\":[36,25,35],\"quotes\":[\"bought for roughly $12 billion\",\"$12.9 billion acquisition of Hugging Face\",\"acquired by Nvidia for $13 billion\"]}\n"}}
